---
import { Image } from "astro:assets"
import MetaPage from "@/components/MetaPage.astro"
import Shot from "@/components/Shot.astro"
import Layout from "@/layouts/Layout.astro"
import plan from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/plan.jpg"
import habits from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/habits.jpg"
import tasks from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/tasks.jpg"
import goals from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/goals.jpg"
import review from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/review.jpg"
import calendar from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/calendar.jpg"
import assistant from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/assistant.jpg"
import mPlan from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/m-plan.jpg"
import mHabits from "@/assets/projects/habit-tracker/m-habits.jpg"

const facts = [
  ["Course", "Software Product Management, ISB"],
  ["Research", "60+ respondents, five streams"],
  ["Users", "42 weekly active at last count"],
  ["Stack", "Next.js, Supabase, IndexedDB, Groq"],
]

const metrics = [
  { k: "AI suggestions rejected", v: "14.1%", note: "North star, lower is better" },
  { k: "Weekly active users", v: "42", note: "Up 35% on the prior week" },
  { k: "Signup conversion", v: "83.3%", note: "Started signup and finished it" },
  { k: "Time to first task", v: "54s", note: "Median, from signup" },
  { k: "Sessions", v: "112", note: "Doubled week on week" },
  { k: "Task completion", v: "33.8%", note: "Down from 51.5%" },
]
---

<Layout>
  <MetaPage slot="head" title="Habit Tracker" description="A personal planner that turns habits, tasks, goals and a calendar into one plan for the day." />
  <article>
    <a class="back" href="/projects">← Projects</a>
    <h1>Habit Tracker</h1>
    <p class="dek">A personal planner that turns habits, tasks, goals and a calendar into one plan for the day.</p>

    <dl class="facts">
      {facts.map(([k, v]) => (<div><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>))}
    </dl>

    <p class="links">
      <a href="https://habit-tracker-acpprad.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Habit tracker ↗</a>
      <a href="https://to-be-done-omega.vercel.app" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">To Be Done ↗</a>
      <a href="https://github.com/ACPpradyuman1/habit-tracker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Source on GitHub ↗</a>
    </p>

    <Shot src={plan} alt="The Plan page showing today's free slots, suggested tasks, goals and events" caption="The Plan page. Free time, habits, tasks and calendar events in one view, with a draft plan for the day already filled in." eager />

    <prose-content>
      <h2>Overview</h2>
      <p>
        This started in ISB's Software Product Management course, where every team takes one product from customer discovery to a live, measured launch inside a single term. Our team of six built To Be Done, a planner that proposes an order for a student's day and lets them throw it out. I kept building on the same idea afterwards, and this habit tracker is where most of that later work went.
      </p>
      <p>
        I was running my own week out of four apps at the time. Habits were in one, tasks in another, goals in a notes file and the actual day in Outlook. None of them knew about the others, so the real planning happened in my head every morning, and it turned out most of the people we spoke to were doing the same.
      </p>

      <h2>Research</h2>
      <p>
        We ran five parallel research streams with more than sixty respondents, about 80% of them students. Three findings came back clearly enough to shape everything else. 93% wanted their calendar pulled in for them, 80% wanted tasks and habits in one view, and 80% wanted to review or override anything the AI suggested, with only about one in five happy to hand it over to autopilot.
      </p>
      <p>
        The pain points were just as consistent. Habits falling off the radar was the most cited problem (12 of 20 in one stream), followed by switching between a calendar and a to-do list (9 of 20). The most wanted outcome was feeling less mentally cluttered (16 of 20), which is why the problem was framed around the cognitive load of deciding, not around organising tasks faster.
      </p>

      <h2>Users</h2>
      <p>
        Three personas came out of the interviews. The Deadline Firefighter has no system at all and decides under pressure, usually in the hours before something is due. The Fragmented Tracker is already organised, just across too many apps. The Tool Skeptic has a system that works and does not want an algorithm near it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The first two became growth targets. The Skeptic was the largest single group in one data set (45%) but scored lowest on trust (2.75 out of 5), so they were left out on purpose and kept as a design constraint instead. If a feature would put off a Skeptic, it would probably put off the other two a little as well.
      </p>

      <h2>Problem</h2>
      <p>
        The shared job was simple to state. Know what to prioritise today, without working it out by hand every morning. Most productivity tools are built around storing things, and very few help with the question of when something should actually get done. A task list does not know about a lecture from ten to eleven thirty, and a calendar does not know the capstone draft is due at five.
      </p>
      <p>
        The second problem was trust. Tools that schedule for you tend to invent time that does not exist or move things without asking. Once that happens a couple of times, the plan is ignored and the tool goes back to being a list.
      </p>

      <h2>Approach</h2>
      <p>
        Everything was organised around one page, called Plan. It reads the calendar, works out the free gaps, and drafts a suggestion for each gap from open tasks and habits. Every suggestion carries a one-line reason. Nothing is booked until it is accepted, and each slot can be scheduled, swapped or left empty.
      </p>
    </prose-content>

    <Shot src={habits} alt="Habits page with habit cards, streaks and a habit chain" caption="Habits, with targets by day, week or a custom range. Habits can be chained, so logging a run prompts the meditation that usually follows it." />

    <prose-content>
      <p>
        The scope was cut deliberately. The first version had to prove the base loop with no AI at all, which meant one place for tasks, habits and calendar events, visible free slots, and manual ranking with a reason attached. Auto-ranking, learned time estimates and adaptive ordering were held back for the next release, along with anything that looked like gamification, team features or payments.
      </p>
    </prose-content>

    <Shot src={tasks} alt="Tasks page with priorities, due dates and subtasks" caption="Tasks, with priorities, due dates, subtasks and time estimates that are compared against actual focus time." />
    <Shot src={goals} alt="Goals page with rollup progress" caption="Goals roll up the habits and tasks linked to them, so progress is measured from real activity." />

    <prose-content>
      <h2>Key decisions</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Suggest from day one.</strong> The original design had a seven-day calibration period, where the model would watch quietly before suggesting anything. It was cut after 40% of one research stream named setup hassle as the main reason they would stop using a tool. That stream had only fifteen responses, the weakest evidence in the whole study, and it still changed the product the most. The rejection of a bad first plan became the calibration instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The AI extracts, it never schedules.</strong> The Assistant lets you talk through a day in plain language, for example "I have class from two to four and want an hour of reading before dinner." The model only pulls out the items, durations and constraints. The actual times are placed by a deterministic packer that can only use real free time, so the plan can be wrong about preference but never about availability.
      </p>
    </prose-content>

    <Shot src={assistant} alt="Assistant page for planning a day in plain language" caption="The Assistant. A plan is proposed in conversation and written to the calendar only after it is confirmed." />

    <prose-content>
      <p>
        <strong>Every rejection is a lesson.</strong> Because each suggestion carries a reason, a rejected one tells the model exactly where it read the day wrong. When a suggestion is overridden, a short note can be added ("gym is always in the morning"), and that note is fed back into the same learned model. The suggestion itself is easy to copy, but a history of a user's disagreements is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Offline first.</strong> Every read and write goes to a local database on the device and syncs in the background. Logging a habit on a train with no signal works instantly, and the change is pushed once the phone is back online.
      </p>

      <h2>Metrics</h2>
      <p>
        The north star was the AI suggestion rejection rate, the share of suggested plan items that users throw out. It was picked because it shows whether the product understands the person using it, and every other metric was arranged underneath it as a driver. Activation was defined as adding a first task, and week-four retention was set as the real test of whether the plan still mattered after onboarding.
      </p>
    </prose-content>

    <div class="metrics">
      {metrics.map((m) => (
        <div>
          <span class="mk">{m.k}</span>
          <span class="mv">{m.v}</span>
          <span class="mn">{m.note}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
    <p class="caption">Live dashboard, week of 8 to 12 August.</p>

    <prose-content>
      <p>
        The numbers were mostly encouraging, with one clear warning. Usage grew quickly, but task completion fell from 51.5% to 33.8% over the same week, which suggests new users were adding more than they finished. Time to first task also went up, from 33 to 54 seconds, so onboarding got slower as more people came through it. Calendar connection was only 16.7%, well short of the 60% target in the spec, and that matters because the plan is only as good as the calendar behind it.
      </p>
    </prose-content>

    <Shot src={calendar} alt="Calendar month view with events and task due dates" caption="Calendar, synced from Outlook or Google feeds. Each event can be broken into a checklist linked to real tasks." />
    <Shot src={review} alt="Weekly review with focus time and habit stats" caption="Review. Habit logs, tasks done and focus time for the week, compared against the previous period." />

    <div class="mobile">
      <Image src={mPlan} alt="Plan page on a phone" widths={[400, 800]} sizes="(width >= 40rem) 14rem, 45vw" />
      <Image src={mHabits} alt="Habits page on a phone" widths={[400, 800]} sizes="(width >= 40rem) 14rem, 45vw" />
    </div>
    <p class="caption">Installed as an app on the phone, where most of the logging happens.</p>

    <prose-content>
      <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
      <p>
        Offline support made the data layer much harder. Two devices can disagree for a while, and a few actions such as calendar sync have to wait for a connection. That was accepted because the alternative was a planner that only works at a desk.
      </p>
      <p>
        The AI was also kept on a short leash, which makes it less impressive in a demo. It will not reshuffle a whole week on its own. A planner is only useful if it is believed, and being right about time matters more than being clever about it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Working professionals were left out of version one. We had exactly one in sixty respondents, and building for them would have meant designing against the thinnest data we had.
      </p>

      <h2>Outcome</h2>
      <p>
        To Be Done went live to our cohort during the course and was presented at Demo Day with real usage data. The cost to serve fits inside free infrastructure tiers, with model inference under ₹4 per active user per month. The habit tracker carried the same thesis further, going from an empty repository to version 1.58 in four weeks across 146 commits.
      </p>

      <h2>Learnings</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest lesson was that trust is a feature. Every suggestion that did not fit the day cost more attention than a good one earned, and that pattern shaped almost every later decision. The second was that subtraction is real product work. Some of the best releases removed things, and the main page got more useful each time it got shorter.
      </p>
      <p>
        The open question is retention. The build was never the risky part. Whether students keep opening it four weeks in, and whether any of them would pay for it, is still to be proven, and the honest fallback if they will not is selling cohort-level insight to programme offices instead.
      </p>
    </prose-content>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .back {
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    text-decoration: none;
  }
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-xs);
  }
  .dek {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    margin-block: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-m);
  }
  .facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-s);
    padding-block: var(--space-s);
    border-block: 1px solid var(--border);
    @media (width >= 40rem) { grid-template-columns: repeat(4, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }
  dt { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--muted-foreground); }
  dd { font-size: var(--step--1); margin: 0; }
  .links {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-m);
    margin-block: var(--space-s) var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }
  prose-content { display: block; }
  prose-content :global(h2) { margin-block-start: var(--space-l); }
  .metrics {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    margin-block-start: var(--space-m);
    @media (width >= 40rem) { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
    div {
      display: flex;
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 0.15rem;
      padding: var(--space-xs) var(--space-s);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 0.5rem;
      background: var(--gray-2);
    }
  }
  .mk { font-size: var(--step--1); color: var(--muted-foreground); }
  .mv { font-size: var(--step-2); font-weight: var(--font-weight-medium); line-height: 1.1; }
  .mn { font-size: var(--step--2, 0.75rem); color: var(--gray-10); }
  .mobile {
    display: flex;
    gap: var(--space-s);
    justify-content: center;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-m);
  }
  .mobile :global(img) {
    inline-size: 45%;
    max-inline-size: 14rem;
    block-size: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 1rem;
  }
  .caption {
    text-align: center;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--muted-foreground);
    margin-block: var(--space-2xs) var(--space-m);
  }
</style>
